    <mytitle|Where we are>

    \;

    Lecture 6, at every lattice <math|N> and upper scale <math|T>:

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<le\>C<rsub|\<gamma\>>*<around*|(|1+p<rsup|5>+\<delta\><rsub|N,T,p>|)>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|N,T,p>=<around|\<\|\|\>|T*Z<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><rsub|L<rsup|2*p><around|(|\<bbb-P\>;L<rsup|2>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Three things remain:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>the boundary term vanishes: <math|T*Z<rsub|T>\<to\>0> at fixed
      <math|N>;

      <item>uniform energy bounds <math|\<Rightarrow\>> compactness of
      processes and measures;

      <item>identify the limit: coefficients, noise, equations.
    </enumerate>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|The subtle point>

      Convergence of the fields does not pass an FBSDE to the limit: the
      backward martingale could acquire <strong|extra noise>. The statistical
      meaning of the backward variable saves us.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <with|font-size|1.6|font-series|bold|color|dark red|The boundary at
    infinite resolution>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture 7>

