  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The deterministic coefficients>

    \;

    Uniform bounds do not identify a limit: a weakly convergent vertex cannot
    in general be contracted against a varying covariance. Three tools:

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Weak vertex measures>: bounded rooted norms
      <math|\<Rightarrow\>> signed measures, weakly compact; the centred
      kernel in a first-moment topology. The limits solve the same fixed
      point, unique in its ball: the whole sequence converges.

      <item><strong|Compact sandwich>: <math|G<rsub|N>\<to\>G> in
      <math|<math-up|HS>>, <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|N>\<to\><math-ss|B>>
      weakly, symmetric, uniformly bounded,
      <math|sup<rsub|N><around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|N><math-ss|B><rsub|N>|\<\|\|\>>\<le\>\<theta\>\<less\>1>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|G<math-ss|B>|\<\|\|\>>\<le\>\<theta\>>
      <math|\<Rightarrow\>> <math|<around|(|I+G<rsub|N><math-ss|B><rsub|N>|)><rsup|-1>*G<rsub|N>\<to\><around|(|I+G<math-ss|B>|)><rsup|-1>*G>
      in <math|<math-up|HS>>.

      <item><strong|Contraction against a moving covariance>: rows of the
      inverses are Hölder <math|+> HS convergence <math|\<Rightarrow\>>
      <math|<around|(|H<rsup|N>|)><rsup|2>\<to\>H<rsup|2>> uniformly; weak
      vertices can be contracted.
    </itemize>

    Hence <math|H<rsup|N><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsup|N><rsub|q>|)>\<to\>H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>
    in <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|I;<math-up|HS>|)>> and
    <math|b<rsup|N><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsup|N><rsub|q>|)>\<to\>b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>
    in <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|I;H|)>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Hidden noise>

    \;

    <math|L<rsup|n><rsub|t>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>g<rsub|n><around|(|s|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>B<rsub|s>>,
    <math|g<rsub|n>=1+sin <around|(|n*s|)>>: integrands <math|\<to\>1>
    weakly, squares <math|\<to\><frac|3|2>>.

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|L<rsup|n>,B|)>\<Longrightarrow\><around|(|B+2<rsup|-1/2>*<wide|B|~>,<space|0.27em>B|)>,<space|2em><wide|B|~>\<perp\>B.
    </equation*>

    <image|<tuple|<#>|hidden_noise.pdf>|0.6par|||>

    Left: <math|<around|(|B<rsub|1>,L<rsup|n><rsub|1>-B<rsub|1>|)>>,
    <math|n=200>: correlation <math|\<approx\>0>, variance of the gap
    <math|\<approx\><frac|1|2>> \V the residual does not vanish and is
    orthogonal to <math|B>. Right: <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>g<rsub|n>\<to\>t>
    but <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>g<rsub|n><rsup|2>\<to\><frac|3|2>*t>.

    A limiting FBSDE obtained this way would carry an <strong|extra
    martingale> orthogonal to the driving noise. We need <strong|strong>
    compactness of the backward integrand
    <math|\<Xi\>=<math-up|Cov>-H<around|(|Y|)>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The Riccati equation of the posterior covariance>

    \;

    <\lemma*>
      <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>=D<rsup|2>*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)>\<ge\>0>
      and

      <\equation*>
        <math-up|dCov><rsub|q>=-<math-up|Cov><rsub|q><rsup|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q+D<rsup|3>*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)><around|[|\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>|]>
      </equation*>
    </lemma*>

    Lecture 4: conditional variance is resolved at rate
    <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t><wide|<with|math-font|cal*|R>|\<dot\>><rsub|t><math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>;
    here <math|\<partial\><rsub|q><with|math-font|cal*|R>=1>.

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|<around|\<langle\>|f,<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>f|\<rangle\>>\<ge\>0>:
      a <strong|nonnegative supermartingale>, initial value
      <math|<math-up|Var><rsub|\<nu\><rsub|N>><around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>,f|\<rangle\>>>
      bounded uniformly (uniform moments, Corollary).

      <item>Stop at level <math|K>: decreasing part and bracket have bounded
      expectations <math|\<Rightarrow\>> time regularity <math|W<rsup|a,1>>,
      <math|a\<less\><frac|1|2>> <math|\<Rightarrow\>> strong compactness of
      each finite block.

      <item>Spatial tails: isometry <math|+> symmetry, and terminal averaging
      <math|\<psi\><rsub|Q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|Q>*<big|int><rsub|L><rsup|L+1><around|(|Y<rsub|s>+Z<rsub|s>|)>\<mathd\>s>
      (<math|H<rsup|\<sigma\>>> bound on <math|Y>,
      <math|\<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<le\>C*s<rsup|-5/2>>).
    </itemize>

    <math|\<Rightarrow\>> <math|<math-up|Cov><rsup|N>> tight in strong
    <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|I;<math-up|HS>|)>>; brackets
    <math|<big|int><math-up|CovCov><rsup|\<ast\>>> and covariations with
    <math|W> converge.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Identification of the martingales>

    \;

    On a common filtered space (Skorokhod), along a subsequence:

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|\<psi\>> is a continuous square-integrable martingale for
      the joint filtration; its bracket and its covariation with <math|W> are
      those of <math|<big|int><math-up|Cov><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W>;

      <item>so <math|\<psi\>-<big|int><math-up|Cov><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W>
      has zero bracket and vanishes: <strong|no extra martingale>;

      <item>same for the forward equation, and
      <math|X=<big|int>C<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W> with the <strong|same>
      <math|W> (needed to pass the thresholded energies).
    </itemize>

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Y<rsub|q>>|<cell|=<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q><around|[|b<around|(|Y|)>+H<around|(|Y|)>*Z|]>\<mathd\>s+<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>H<around|(|Y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W,>>|<row|<cell|Z<rsub|q>>|<cell|=-<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q><around|[|b<around|(|Y|)>+H<around|(|Y|)>*Z|]>\<mathd\>s+<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>\<Xi\><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W,<space|2em>X<rsub|q>=<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>C<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The result (I): convergence and the limiting FBSDE>

    \;

    <\theorem*>
      <dueto|subsequential construction>For <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>,
      <math|q<rsub|0>> large, along a subsequence of lattices:

      <\enumerate-alpha>
        <item><math|<around|(|Y<rsup|N>,Z<rsup|N>,\<psi\><rsup|N>,X<rsup|N>|)>>
        converge in <math|C*<around|(|I;H|)>>, <math|W<rsup|N>> in
        <math|C*<around|(|I;H<rsup|-r>|)>>, <math|r\<gtr\><frac|3|2>>,
        <math|<around|(|<math-up|Cov><rsup|N>,\<Xi\><rsup|N>|)>> in strong
        <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|I;<math-up|HS>|)>>, jointly, a.s. on a new
        space;

        <item>the limit solves the FBSDE on
        <math|<around|[|q<rsub|0>,\<infty\>|)>> with the continuum
        coefficients <math|b,H> (unique in their ball), an
        <math|H>-cylindrical Brownian motion obtained as the limit of the
        lattice innovations, <math|<math-up|Cov>=H<around|(|Y|)>+\<Xi\>\<ge\>0>,
        and <math|\<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<le\>C*q<rsup|-5/2>>;
      </enumerate-alpha>
    </theorem*>

    <math|\<psi\><rsup|N>> is tight in <math|C*<around|(|I;H|)>> because its
    brackets <math|<big|int><math-up|Cov><rsup|N><math-up|Cov><rsup|N\<ast\>>>
    are tight and its Fourier tails are uniformly small.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The result (II): the terminal law>

    \;

    <\theorem*>
      <dueto|subsequential construction, continued>

      <\enumerate-alpha>
        <assign|enumi-nr|2>

        <item><math|Y<rsub|q>\<to\>\<Phi\>> in <math|H<rsup|-s>>,
        <math|s\<gtr\><frac|1|2>>, a.s. and in mean square;
        <math|<math-up|Law><around|(|\<Phi\>|)>=\<nu\>> is the weak limit of
        <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>>, and <math|<around|\<langle\>|Y<rsub|q>+Z<rsub|q>,h|\<rangle\>>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>,h|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<cal-F\><rsub|q>|]>>;

        <item>the limiting thresholded energies (built with
        <math|X=<big|int>C<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W>) obey the bounds of UV
        stability.
      </enumerate-alpha>
    </theorem*>

    The posterior structure survives: <math|\<psi\>=Y+Z> is a <strong|closed
    observable>, <math|Y> is computed from the explicit action, <math|Z> is
    small at high resolution.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The renormalised field equation>

    \;

    <\theorem*>
      <dueto|integration by parts><math|G<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=g<around|(|<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,h<rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>,\<ldots\>,<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,h<rsub|k>|\<rangle\>>|)>>,
      <math|g\<in\>C<rsub|b><rsup|\<infty\>>>, <math|h,h<rsub|i>> smooth:

      <\equation*>
        lim<rsub|N> \<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|G*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|N>*\<Phi\><rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>|)><around|\<langle\>|\<mu\><rsub|N>*\<Phi\><rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>+\<lambda\><around|(|\<Phi\><rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>|)><rsup|3>,j<rsub|N>*h|\<rangle\>>|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<around*|[|D<rsub|h>*G<around|(|\<Phi\>|)>-G<around|(|\<Phi\>|)><around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>,<around|(|q<rsub|0>-\<Delta\>|)>*h|\<rangle\>>|]>.
      </equation*>
    </theorem*>

    Proof: lattice Gaussian integration by parts in
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|N>-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,K<rsub|q<rsub|0>>*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>>;
    <math|K<rsub|q<rsub|0>>*j<rsub|N>*h=j<rsub|N>*<around|(|q<rsub|0>-\<Delta\><rsup|<math-up|tr>><rsub|N>|)>*h>;
    tightness and uniform moments.

    <\itemize>
      <item>The terminal case of the DS equations of Lecture 4.

      <item>The renormalised cube <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>*\<Phi\>+\<lambda\>*\<Phi\><rsup|3>>
      is identified only <strong|tested in expectation>, not as a random
      distribution.

      <item>Consistent with the expectation (Lecture 4) that the limiting
      force is an observable without terminal representative; this is
      <strong|not proved>.

      <item>At every finite resolution the force is explicit:
      <math|F<rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*Z<rsub|q>-D*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|What is not proved>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Uniqueness> of the limit, of the FBSDE, independence of
      the regularisation.

      <item><strong|Small mass / strong coupling>: the construction needs
      <math|\<gamma\>*q<rsub|0><rsup|-1/2>> small, i.e. weak coupling
      <math|\<lambda\>/m\<ll\>1>. Removing it needs a priori estimates
      without the small feedback <math|\<gamma\><rsup|2>/q<rsub|0>>, and a
      coefficient construction outside the perturbative ball.

      <item><strong|Axioms>: reflection positivity holds on the lattice
      (lattice and mid-lattice planes) and passes to <math|\<nu\>> for every
      coordinate reflection (standard; exercise); rotation invariance and
      clustering need infinite volume.

      <item><strong|Infinite volume>: the force and its Burgers flow are
      intensive; estimates must be made local. Uniform-in-volume coefficient
      bounds and local energy moments are open; retaining the full FBSDE also
      needs control of the <strong|noise from infinity> in the nonlocal
      <math|\<Xi\>>.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Summary of the course>

    \;

    Wightman <math|\<to\>> Euclidean (L1) <math|\<to\>> free field (L2)
    <math|\<to\>> stochastic quantisation, scale decomposition (L3)
    <math|\<to\>> Polchinski, FBSDE, variational problem (L4) <math|\<to\>>
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> through the FBSDE in forecast coordinates
    (L5\U7).

    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|6|6|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Lecture
    4 (formal)>|<cell|Lectures 5\U7>|<cell|in the limit>>|<row|<cell|forecast
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*\<Phi\><rsub|T>>>|<cell|<math|\<psi\>=Y+Z>>|<cell|closed
    martingale>>|<row|<cell|response <math|H<rsub|t>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>>|<cell|<math|<math-up|Cov>=H<around|(|Y|)>+\<Xi\>>>|<cell|strong
    <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|<math-up|HS>|)>>,
    Riccati>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>,
    Wetterich>|<cell|<math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> explicit, defect
    <math|e>>|<cell|deterministic coefficients>>|<row|<cell|force
    <math|F<rsub|t>>>|<cell|<math|K<rsub|q>*Z<rsub|q>-D*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>>|<cell|observable
    at finite <math|q>>>|<row|<cell|DS equation>|<cell|integration by
    parts>|<cell|tested in expectation>>>>>

    <strong|UV stability> <math|=> an explicit action predicts the
    conditional mean up to an error that is small at high resolution.
  </hidden>>
